      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Heat the lard in a dutch oven over medium-high heat. Brown the chicken thighs skin-side down for 5 minutes until golden. Flip and cook for 2 more minutes. Remove and set aside."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Reduce the heat to medium. Cook the onion in the fat left in the pot for 10 minutes until soft and golden. Add the garlic and cook for 1 minute."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Remove from the heat. Stir in the paprika and tomato puree. Return to medium heat and cook for 30 seconds. Pour in the stock and add the bay leaves."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Return the chicken, skin-side up. Bring to a simmer, cover, and cook on low heat for 35 minutes until the chicken is cooked through."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Whisk the flour into the sour cream until smooth. Remove the pot from the heat. Stir the sour cream mixture into the sauce. Return to a very low heat for 2 minutes, stirring gently. Do not boil. Discard bay leaves. Scatter over parsley and serve with egg noodles."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "problem": "Sour cream sauce has curdled"
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Always add it off the heat, and whisk the flour into the cream before adding to stabilise it. Never let it boil after adding."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "problem": "Sauce is orange rather than deep red"
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "You need more paprika. Three tablespoons for four servings sounds like a lot but is correct for an authentic Hungarian colour and flavour."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "problem": "Chicken skin is soggy"
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "The skin softens in the braise. If you want crisp skin, uncover the pot for the last 10 minutes or briefly place under a grill before serving."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Chicken paprikash is one of the defining dishes of Hungarian cuisine. It appears on family tables and restaurant menus in equal measure across Hungary. The dish was carried across the world by Hungarian emigrants and is now recognised internationally. Egg noodles called nokedli are the traditional accompaniment."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
